\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The reference implementation of the \$t\^{o}kEx{} in the t\^{o}k system, shown against a fictional foreign currency, the TIK. \emph  {Top:} the market view --- the current exchange rate, each account's balance (here $100$~t\^{o}ks and $1000$~TIK), and the two exchange velocities, quoted per $15$~days (the reference pace $\dot  {R}$, one t\^{o}k per $15$~days). \emph  {Bottom:} the same participant declaring their two inputs, an estimate ($1$~TIK${}={}10$~t\^{o}ks) and a degree of confidence ($75\%$), with the response curves the mechanism derives from them.}}{18}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{figRefImplUI}{{7}{18}{The reference implementation of the \StokEx {} in the t\^{o}k system, shown against a fictional foreign currency, the TIK. \emph {Top:} the market view --- the current exchange rate, each account's balance (here $100$~t\^{o}ks and $1000$~TIK), and the two exchange velocities, quoted per $15$~days (the reference pace $\dot {R}$, one t\^{o}k per $15$~days). \emph {Bottom:} the same participant declaring their two inputs, an estimate ($1$~TIK${}={}10$~t\^{o}ks) and a degree of confidence ($75\%$), with the response curves the mechanism derives from them}{figure.caption.12}{}}
